  "issue_number": 201,
  "issue_title": "[bug] verify_benchmark crashes (traceback) on tampered/corrupt input instead of reporting FAIL",
  "issue_body": "**Describe the bug**\n\n`scripts/verify_benchmark.py` \u2014 the hidden-benchmark integrity verifier \u2014 crashes with an uncaught traceback on exactly the tamper/corruption cases it exists to detect, instead of reporting a clean `FAIL`.\n\nTwo independent paths:\n\n**1. Undecryptable / tampered split, or wrong password \u2192 uncaught exception in `load_splits`.**\n\n```python\n# load_splits, ~line 77\ndata = _decrypt_file(fp, password)   # no try/except\n```\n\n`load_splits`'s own docstring promises a decode problem is \"recorded as a problem (the splits dict is still returned so the manifest check can run on what decoded)\". But a tampered ciphertext or a wrong `BENCHMARK_PASSWORD` raises `cryptography.exceptions.InvalidTag` (AES-GCM auth failure) \u2014 or `binascii.Error` on bad base64 \u2014 which propagates uncaught through `verify_dir` to `main()`. The single most important case for an integrity verifier (a modified `eval.json`) dies with a stack trace rather than a `FAIL \u2014 decryption failed` line.\n\n**2. Missing / corrupt `meta.json` \u2192 uncaught exception.**\n\n```python\n# main(), ~line 146 \u2014 always runs, even when verify_dir already reported the problem\nproblems = verify_dir(args.dir, args.password)\nmeta = json.loads((Path(args.dir) / \"meta.json\").read_text())   # FileNotFoundError / JSONDecodeError\n```\n\n`verify_dir` deliberately handles a missing manifest (`return [\"missing meta.json\"]`), but `main()` then re-reads `meta.json` unconditionally *before* the `if problems:` reporting block \u2014 so a deleted or truncated `meta.json` (a tamper case) throws before the clean report can print. (And `verify_dir`/`verify_meta_file` themselves `json.loads(meta)` with no guard, so a *corrupt-but-present* `meta.json` crashes them too.)\n\n**Expected vs actual**\n\n*Expected:* a tampered ciphertext, wrong password, or missing/corrupt `meta.json` produces a structured `FAIL [...] \u2014 N problem(s)` and `sys.exit(1)`.\n\n*Actual:* an uncaught `InvalidTag` / `FileNotFoundError` / `JSONDecodeError` traceback.\n\n**To reproduce** (offline, no GPU/network):\n\n```python\n# corrupt meta.json in --dir mode:\nPath(d, \"meta.json\").write_text(\"{ not valid json\")\nverify_dir(d, \"pw\")          # -> raises json.JSONDecodeError (should report a problem)\n\n# undecryptable split:\nPath(d, \"eval.json\").write_text(\"!!! not base64 !!!\")\nload_splits(d, \"pw\")         # -> raises (should record \"eval.json: decryption failed\")\n```\n\n**Why it matters**\n\nThis tool is the \"missing verifier\" that makes the builder's promise \u2014 *\"prove the benchmark has not been modified since creation\"* \u2014 real. A verifier that stack-traces on tampered input fails at its one job: a maintainer running it in CI against a tampered benchmark sees a Python crash (easy to misread as a tooling error) instead of a clear integrity failure.\n\n**Proposed fix**\n\nMake every tamper/corruption path a recorded problem, not an exception:\n- wrap `_decrypt_file` in `load_splits` and append `\"<file>: decryption failed (...)\"` on any failure, continuing so the manifest check still runs on what decoded (as the docstring already promises);\n- read `meta.json` through a small helper that returns `(meta, problem)` for missing/corrupt input, used by `verify_dir` and `verify_meta_file`;\n- in `main()`, drop the unconditional re-read and read `meta.json` only on the success path (where it is guaranteed valid).\n\nAdd offline tests for undecryptable splits, wrong password, and missing/corrupt meta. Happy to open a PR.\n\n**Environment**\n- Python version: 3.11\n- GPU: CPU only (bug reproduces offline)\n- Commit hash: `f4aa3cb`\n",
  "merged_at": "2026-07-14T18:38:03.000Z",
